.Dd October 6, 2026
.Dt RELIC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm relic
.Nd evaluate Datalog programs and answer queries
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl aceinsv
.Op Fl F Ar format
.Op Fl I Ar import
.Op Fl j Ar threads
.Op Fl l Ar limit Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl O Ar export
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Op Fl q Ar query
.Op Fl T Ar types
.Op Fl X Ar debug
.Op Ar
.Nm
.Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility loads a Datalog program, checks it, and answers its queries.
The program is read from each
.Ar file
in the order given, all of them forming one program, or from the standard
input when no
.Ar file
is given.
A program or a table that would be read from standard input when it is a
terminal is a usage error instead, since
.Nm
would wait for it with nothing said; the terminal is for
.Fl i
to read.
Facts may also be imported from CSV and TSV tables with
.Fl I ,
as described in
.Sx IMPORTS ,
and relations written to files with
.Fl O ,
as described in
.Sx EXPORTS .
.Pp
A program is a sequence of facts, rules and queries.
A fact is an atom ending in a period, and holds no variable; a rule is an
atom, the
.Ql :-
token and a body of literals separated by commas, ending in a period; a
query is a body ending in a question mark.
An atom is a predicate name and at most 32 arguments in parentheses, which
may be left out when there are none, as in
.Ql done. :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
edge(a, b).
edge(b, c).
path(X, Y) :- edge(X, Y).
path(X, Z) :- edge(X, Y), path(Y, Z).
path(a, X)?
.Ed
.Pp
Names are ASCII letters, digits and underscores.
A symbol begins with a lowercase letter, and a variable with an uppercase
letter or an underscore.
The characters
.Ql #
and
.Ql $
are reserved, and are errors outside a string or a comment.
.Ql _
alone matches anything in an atom of a body, and names no value, so it
cannot stand in a rule's head or a comparison.
Under
.Ql not
it is a column the atom does not test:
.Ql not order(_, C, _)
holds when no order has
.Ar C
in its second column, and
.Ql not p(_, _)
holds when
.Ar p
is empty.
Integers are decimal, may be negative, and must fit in 64 bits.
Digits followed by a period and a digit, as in
.Ql 1.5 ,
or by an exponent, as in
.Ql 1e5 ,
are an error: there are no floating-point numbers, and
.Ql 1.5
is reserved for them.
Strings are written in double quotes on one line and may hold any bytes,
spelled with the escapes
.Ql \e\e ,
.Ql \e\(dq ,
.Ql \en ,
.Ql \et ,
.Ql \er ,
.Ql \e0
and
.Ql \exHH .
A body literal is an atom, an atom preceded by
.Ql not ,
or a comparison with one of
.Ql = ,
.Ql != ,
.Ql < ,
.Ql <= ,
.Ql >
and
.Ql >= .
Values of one type compare as
.Fl s
sorts them: integers numerically, and symbols and strings bytewise.
A comparison that orders values of different types is false.
.Ql %
begins a comment that runs to the end of the line.
.Pp
Either side of a comparison may be an integer expression: integers and
variables combined with
.Ql + ,
.Ql \- ,
.Ql * ,
.Ql /
and
.Ql mod ,
a unary minus and parentheses.
.Ql * ,
.Ql /
and
.Ql mod
bind tighter than
.Ql +
and
.Ql \- ,
a unary minus tighter than both, and every binary operator groups to the
left.
Arithmetic is on 64-bit integers: division truncates toward zero, and the
remainder takes the sign of the dividend.
An expression is at most 32 levels deep, each operand a level and each
operator over it one more, so a chain such as
.Ql X + 1 + 1
joins at most 32 operands, and parentheses nest at most 32 deep.
An expression stands only in a comparison, not in an atom's argument or a
rule's head.
An equality one side of which is a variable nothing else binds before it,
and the other side of which reads only bound variables, assigns the
variable that value, whichever side it is written on:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dist(X, Y, 1) :- edge(X, Y).
dist(X, Z, D1) :- dist(X, Y, D), edge(Y, Z), D < 8, D1 = D + 1.
.Ed
.Pp
A symbol or string constant written as an operand, as in
.Ql X + foo ,
rejects the program when it is read.
An operand that is a variable holding a symbol or a string gives the
expression no value, and a comparison or assignment over it is false, as an
ordering of different types is.
A result outside the 64-bit integers, a division or remainder by zero, and
the remainder of \-9223372036854775808 by \-1 are errors instead: the
evaluation stops, its query writes no rows, and
.Nm
exits with a nonzero status.
Whether an evaluation meets such a result depends on the bindings it
evaluates the expression over, so a query answered through its rewrite may
succeed where evaluating the whole program, with
.Fl a ,
fails.
A rule computing a new integer from one it derived, such as
.Ql n(Y) :- n(X), Y = X + 1 ,
derives until it overflows; bound it with a comparison, as
.Ql D < 8
bounds
.Ql dist
above.
.Pp
An expression may also call one of six string functions wherever an
operand stands:
.Bl -tag -width "substr(S, I, N)"
.It Fn length S
the number of bytes in
.Ar S ;
.It Fn cat A B
the bytes of
.Ar A
followed by those of
.Ar B ;
.It Fn substr S I
the bytes of
.Ar S
from position
.Ar I
on;
.It Fn substr S I N
at most
.Ar N
bytes of
.Ar S
from position
.Ar I ;
.It Fn index S T
the first position at which the bytes of
.Ar T
occur in
.Ar S ,
or \-1 when they do not;
.It Fn match S R
the first position at which the regular expression
.Ar R
matches in
.Ar S ,
or \-1 when it does not;
.It Fn str V
.Ar V
as a string: a string itself, a symbol's name, or an integer in decimal.
.El
.Pp
A string is its bytes and nothing more:
.Ql length
counts bytes rather than characters, a position is a byte offset counted
from 0, and
.Ql substr
can cut a character of several bytes in half.
.Ql substr
keeps the positions of
.Ar S
from
.Ar I ,
up to
.Ar I Ns + Ns Ar N ,
that lie in the string, so every integer is a valid argument:
.Ql substr("abc", \-1, 2)
is
.Ql \(dqa\(dq
and
.Ql substr("abc", 5)
is empty.
The empty string occurs in every string at position 0, and the empty
pattern matches there.
Tests are comparisons over what the functions give:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
unsafe(P) :- path(P), index(P, "..") >= 0.
object(S, O) :- source(S), substr(S, length(S) - 2) = ".c",
    O = cat(substr(S, 0, length(S) - 2), ".o").
test_file(P) :- path(P), match(P, "^tests/.*\e\e.c$") >= 0.
.Ed
.Pp
An argument that is not of the type the function takes, a symbol or an
integer where it takes a string, or anything but an integer where it takes
a position or a count, is rejected when the program is read if it is a
constant, as in
.Ql length(foo) ;
a variable holding one when the call is evaluated makes the call no value,
and a comparison or assignment over it false.
.Ql str
takes any value, and
.Ql str(foo)
is the string
.Qq foo .
A body literal that begins with a call is a comparison when an operator or
a comparator follows the call's closing parenthesis, and an atom otherwise,
so a predicate may be named
.Ql length
and read as
.Ql length(X, N) .
A string longer than 4294967294 bytes is an error, as an integer outside
the 64-bit integers is, and so is a pattern that is not a regular
expression: one written as a string constant rejects the program when it is
loaded, and one read from a variable stops the evaluation that meets it.
A rule computing a new string from one it derived, such as
.Ql s(Y) :- s(X), Y = cat(X, \(dqa\(dq) ,
derives until a limit stops it; bound it with a comparison, as
.Ql length(X) < 20
would.
.Pp
The regular expressions are those of
.Xr grep 1
.Fl E ,
over bytes, less counted repetition, character classes and
back-references:
.Ql \&.
matches any byte,
.Ql [set]
one byte of the set and
.Ql [^set]
one byte not in it, a set holding bytes and ranges such as
.Ql a\-z ,
.Ql |
separates alternatives,
.Ql * ,
.Ql +
and
.Ql \&?
repeat what precedes them any number of times, at least once and at most
once, parentheses group,
.Ql ^
and
.Ql $
match at the start and the end of the string, and a backslash makes the
byte after it stand for itself, inside a set too.
A
.Ql \&]
first in a set is a member of it, a set ends at the next, so
.Ql [[:alpha:]]
is a set of six bytes followed by
.Ql \&] ,
and
.Ql {
and
.Ql }
are ordinary bytes.
A string constant's escapes are decoded before the pattern is read, so a
pattern written as a constant doubles its backslashes, as
.Ql \(dq\e\e.c$\(dq
does above.
Parentheses nest at most 32 deep.
A match takes time linear in the string for a given pattern; no pattern
makes it backtrack.
.Pp
An aggregate folds the rows of a body in braces into one value, and is
written only as the right side of an equality whose left side is a
variable:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dep_count(T, N) :- task(T), N = count { depends(T, D) }.
dir_size(Dir, S) :- dir(Dir),
    S = sum(Size) { file(F, Dir), size(F, Size) }.
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql count
is the number of rows,
.Ql sum(V)
the total of
.Ar V
over them, and
.Ql min(V)
and
.Ql max(V)
its least and greatest value, in the order comparisons use.
The rows are the distinct values of the variables that occur only inside
the braces, the variable of
.Ql sum ,
.Ql min
or
.Ql max
among them, which must be named rather than
.Ql _ ,
so
.Ql count { edge(a, Y) }
counts the distinct
.Ar Y
and
.Ql count { edge(a, _) }
is 1 when there is any, since
.Ql _
names no column.
A variable that also occurs outside the braces is shared: the body is
folded once for each of its values, and it must be bound by a positive
atom or an assignment both inside the braces and outside them, as
.Ar T
is above.
A group with no rows has a count and a sum of 0, and no
.Ql min
or
.Ql max ,
so the literal is false.
A sum over a value that is not an integer, and a
.Ql min
or
.Ql max
over values of different types, have no value either.
A sum is exact over the whole group, whatever order its rows are met in,
and an error only when the total is outside the 64-bit integers.
The body holds atoms, negated atoms, comparisons and assignments, but no
aggregate, and an aggregate may not read a relation that depends on the
rule it is in.
The four names, and
.Ql mod ,
are symbols and predicate names everywhere else;
.Ql not
names no predicate.
A query's columns are its variables outside any braces, at most 32 of
them.
A rule or a query names at most 64 distinct variables, not counting those
that occur only inside an aggregate's braces, and the braces of an
aggregate name at most 32, those shared with the rule included.
.Pp
Every variable of a rule's head and of a comparison, and every named
variable of a negated literal, must also occur in a positive atom of the
body or be assigned by an equality or an aggregate, and a program may not
be recursive through negation or an aggregate.
A program that breaks either rule is rejected and nothing is evaluated.
A variable a rule names only once, and a predicate the program reads and
never defines, are reported as warnings, which change nothing else; a
variable whose name begins with an underscore is not warned about.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Evaluate the whole program before answering any query, rather than answering
each query through a rewrite that derives only what its constants can
reach.
The answers are the same.
.It Fl c
Check the program and its queries, and evaluate nothing.
Every table named with
.Fl I
is read and checked too, and every export of
.Fl O
is checked and not written.
It cannot be combined with
.Fl a
or
.Fl i .
.It Fl e
Write the plan of everything evaluated to the standard error: each part of
the program as it is planned, and each query's rewrite and plan before its
rows.
A rewrite abandoned over its limit is marked by a line
.Ql abandoned magic rewrite of ,
after which the whole program is planned as though there were none.
A part whose predicates have facts counts them as
.Ql facts: N ;
a predicate of facts alone needs no evaluation and is not shown.
The text is meant to be read and may change from one release to the next.
A plan names a query's answer
.Ql $q
and a serial counting every query prepared, from zero, which need not be the
number diagnostics and
.Fl v
give it.
.It Fl F Ar format
Write query results in
.Ar format :
.Cm text ,
the default,
.Cm csv ,
.Cm tsv
or
.Cm json .
The formats are described in
.Sx OUTPUT .
.It Fl I Ar import
Add a fact for each record of a table, after the program is loaded.
The option may be repeated, and the tables are read in the order given.
.Ar import
is described in
.Sx IMPORTS .
.It Fl i
Once the queries have run, read queries from the standard input and answer
each in turn, as described in
.Sx INTERACTIVE MODE .
The standard input is then never read as a program, and with no
.Ar file
the program is empty.
A run that fails before the session would begin ends without one.
.It Fl j Ar threads
Share the work of evaluation, and of reading the tables
.Fl I
imports, among as many as
.Ar threads
threads, the one running
.Nm
included; 0 asks for one per processor online, up to 256, and the default
is 1.
A round of evaluation is shared only when it holds enough work to repay the
threads, so a program of small rounds runs as it would on one thread, and a
table is shared only among eight threads or more, only when it is read
from a regular file, and only when reading its first 2 MiB does not reach
its end.
The answers, their order and diagnostics are the same whatever the number
of threads.
On successful completion, every counter
.Fl X Cm stats
writes but the parallel rounds agrees across thread counts.
Interruption depends on timing and can leave different retained prefixes
and counters.
How much of its table a refused import has read may also differ.
.Ar threads
is at most 256.
.It Fl l Ar limit Ns = Ns Ar value
Set a resource limit.
The option may be repeated, and a limit given twice takes the later value.
The limits are described in
.Sx RESOURCE LIMITS .
.It Fl n
Run every query and write none of its rows.
The output is never opened, so
.Fl o
creates no file; the exports of
.Fl O
are still written.
With
.Fl v
this counts each query's answers; with
.Fl e
it shows what each query runs.
.It Fl O Ar export
Write every tuple of a relation to a file, once the queries have run.
The option may be repeated, and the relations are written in the order
given.
.Ar export
is described in
.Sx EXPORTS .
.It Fl o Ar file
Write query results to
.Ar file ,
created or truncated, instead of the standard output.
The file is created or truncated only once a query has been answered, or
once a run with no query succeeds, so a run that fails before then, in
evaluating its first query at a limit included, leaves an existing file
alone.
It may not be the same file as any other the run reads or writes, as
.Sx EXPORTS
describes.
.It Fl q Ar query
Answer
.Ar query ,
written as in a program and ending in a question mark, instead of the
queries the program contains.
The option may be repeated, and the queries are answered in the order
given; diagnostics about the third are named
.Ql <query 3> .
The program's own queries are still checked.
.It Fl s
Sort each query's rows before writing them, column by column: integers
before symbols before strings, integers numerically, and symbols and
strings bytewise.
Without
.Fl s
the order of the rows is unspecified, though the same on every run over the
same input.
.It Fl T Ar types
Give the column types of a predicate's imports, written
.Ar predicate : Ns Ar column Ns Op , Ns Ar column ...
and described in
.Sx IMPORTS .
The option may be repeated, once for each predicate.
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl v
Say more on the standard error.
Once: after each query that succeeds, a line
.Ql query N: M rows ,
or
.Ql row
when M is 1,
where N is the query's number: its position among the
.Fl q
queries, or else the program's own, continued in an interactive session as
.Sx INTERACTIVE MODE
describes; and after each export that succeeds, a line
.Ql export N: M rows ,
N its position among the
.Fl O
exports.
Twice: also a note for the first comparison or aggregate in each rule, and
in each query each time it is answered, that had no value and was easily
unintended: a comparison that ordered values of
different types, applied an operator to a symbol or a string, or called a
string function with an argument of a type it does not take, which is
false, or an aggregate whose rows were not all integers or not all of one
type.
.It Fl X Ar debug
Select an internal behaviour, for testing and debugging.
The option may be repeated.
These are not part of the stable interface:
.Bl -tag -width no-reorder
.It Cm group-rules
compile the body of every aggregate into a rule of its own whose relation
the aggregate folds, even a body of one atom whose arguments are distinct
variables, which otherwise is folded over the atom's relation where it
stands;
.It Cm naive
evaluate recursion by the naive fixed point;
.It Cm no-batch-import
read every table of
.Fl I
a field at a time, typing and interning each field and staging each record
as it is read, whatever the number of threads
.Fl j
gives and even with
.Cm parallel ,
as a small table, one read on fewer than eight threads or one read from
anything but a regular file always is, rather
than a window of records at a time; the facts, the values and a refused
import's message are the same, but a refused import may have read less of
its table;
.It Cm no-exists
plan an atom that binds nothing the rest of its rule reads, its other
arguments
.Ql _
or variables the rule names nowhere else, as a scan or a lookup that
continues the join once per tuple it matches, rather than as a test that
continues it at most once;
.It Cm no-factor
rewrite every bound recursive predicate by the ordinary Magic Sets
construction, deriving the answers of every state its demand reaches,
rather than by walking the states reachable from each requested binding and
answering for that binding alone;
.It Cm no-index
build no index, scanning instead;
.It Cm no-magic
answer every query from the whole program;
.It Cm no-reorder
join a rule's atoms in the order they are written, even the one reading
what the previous round derived;
.It Cm no-stream
gather every query's answer whole before writing its first row, as a query
whose plan may derive a row twice is answered, rather than writing the rows
of one whose plan derives each row once as they are derived;
.It Cm parallel
run every round of evaluation that could run on several threads that way,
however little work it holds and however many threads
.Fl j
gives: its rules' derivations gathered as they run and inserted when the
round ends, in the order they would have been inserted as they were
derived; and read every table
.Fl I
imports from a regular file a window of records at a time, as a large
table is read on several threads, however small it is;
.It Cm stats
write the evaluation counters to the standard error when the run ends,
whether or not it succeeded;
the derivations count what rules derive and the facts copied into a
predicate that rules also derive, not the facts of a predicate no rule
derives, and the parallel rounds the rounds whose work was shared among
threads.
.El
.Pp
Each gives the same answers as the default, though two strategies may hold
different amounts while they compute them, and so one may meet a limit
.Fl l
sets that the other does not.
.El
.Pp
An invocation checks everything before it evaluates anything: every file is
loaded and every query prepared, and all the mistakes found are reported,
before the first query runs.
The queries then run in turn, and the first that fails ends the run.
.Sh IMPORTS
An
.Ar import
is written as a path alone,
.Bd -literal -offset indent
path
.Ed
.Pp
or with a head that names what the path does not:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
format[,header]:predicate[:column[,column...]]=path
.Ed
.Pp
and adds a fact of
.Ar predicate
for each record of the table at
.Ar path ,
or of the standard input when
.Ar path
is
.Ql - .
A path alone is read in the format its file name's extension names,
.Ql .csv
or
.Ql .tsv ,
as facts of the predicate the rest of the file name names, without a
header:
.Ql data/edge.tsv
adds facts of
.Ql edge .
The standard input, and a table whose file name says neither, is named with
a head, whose path is everything after the first
.Ql = .
An operand holding a
.Ql =
always has a head, so a path holding one is named with a head too; a
script importing file names it did not choose should give each a head.
.Pp
The column types belong to the predicate.
An import that lists none takes those of the predicate's
.Fl T ,
written
.Ql predicate:column[,column...] ,
given before or after it; with neither, the import is a usage error.
An import may list its own, for a table with other fields to skip, but
every list given for one predicate must keep columns of the same types in
the same order.
The predicate's arity is the number of columns not skipped, at most 32.
A table's facts are added all together or not at all: any mistake in it,
reported with its line and column, adds none of them.
.Pp
The
.Ar format
is
.Cm csv
or
.Cm tsv ,
each read as the output format of the same name writes it, so that the
results of one query written with
.Fl F
read back as the facts they came from.
In both, a record ends at a line feed, or at a carriage return followed by a
line feed; the last needs no line end; an empty line is a record of one empty
field; and every record has one field per column.
Bytes are data: no encoding is assumed and no space is trimmed.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm csv
Fields are separated by commas.
A field that begins with a double quote ends at the next quote not doubled;
inside it a doubled quote is one quote, and commas and line ends are data,
and a comma or a line end must follow it.
A quote inside an unquoted field is an error.
.It Cm tsv
Fields are separated by tabs.
A backslash begins one of the escapes
.Ql \e\e ,
.Ql \et ,
.Ql \en
and
.Ql \er ;
any other escape is an error.
.El
.Pp
Outside a quoted field, a carriage return that does not end a line is an
error.
With
.Cm header ,
the first record is a header of one field per column, and is not imported;
a table with no header is an error.
.Pp
Each
.Ar column
gives the type of its field, which is never guessed:
.Bl -tag -width integer
.It Cm symbol
a symbol, written as in a program;
.It Cm string
a string of the field's bytes, whatever they are;
.It Cm integer
a decimal integer, optionally negative, that fits in 64 bits;
.It Cm _
no value: the field is read and left out of the fact.
.El
.Pp
The symbol
.Ql ann
and the string
.Ql \(dqann\(dq
are different values, so a column declared with the wrong one of the two
joins with nothing.
.Pp
The standard input has one reader: the program, with no
.Ar file ,
the session under
.Fl i ,
or one import of
.Ql - .
When an import or the session reads it, the program is the
.Ar file
operands, and with none it is empty.
Two readers of the standard input are a usage error.
.Sh EXPORTS
An
.Ar export
is written as a path alone,
.Bd -literal -offset indent
path
.Ed
.Pp
or with a head that names what the path does not:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
format:predicate[/arity]=path
.Ed
.Pp
and writes every tuple of
.Ar predicate
to the file at
.Ar path ,
created or truncated.
A path alone is written in the format its file name's extension names,
.Ql .csv ,
.Ql .tsv ,
.Ql .json
or
.Ql .dl ,
and holds the predicate the rest of the file name names:
.Ql out/path.tsv
writes
.Ql path
as
.Cm tsv .
A file name that says neither is named with a head, whose path is
everything after the first
.Ql = .
The standard output is not a path an export writes; a query writes it.
.Pp
No output may be the same file as anything else the run reads or writes.
An export or the
.Fl o
file may not be a program
.Ar file ,
an imported table, the file the standard input is redirected from when the
program, a table or an interactive session reads it, the file the standard
output is redirected to when results or a session's replies go there, or
another output.
Each is a usage error, reported before anything is read or written.
Files are compared by what they are rather than how they are spelled, so
.Ql out.tsv ,
.Ql ./out.tsv ,
a symbolic link to it and a hard link to it are one file, and a file that
does not exist yet is the name it would be created under in its directory.
A device such as
.Pa /dev/null
holds nothing an output could replace, and any number of outputs may write
one.
Two names of a file that does not exist yet are not caught as one when they
differ only in case, or in Unicode normalization, on a file system that
ignores the difference, or when one is a symbolic link to the other.
.Pp
A predicate is a name and an arity together.
An export with no
.Ar arity
writes the one predicate the program has of that name; a name the program
has at several arities is an error that asks for a head naming one, and a
name it does not have at all is an error too.
.Pp
What an export writes is exactly what a query of the predicate with a
variable in every column writes, under the same
.Fl a
and
.Fl s ,
in the format the export names:
.Cm csv ,
.Cm tsv
and
.Cm json
as described in
.Sx OUTPUT ,
with no header, and
.Cm dl ,
each row a fact written as a program writes it,
.Ql path(a, \(dqAnn Lee\(dq).
A
.Cm dl
file loads back as a program holding the same relation, every value with
its type; a
.Cm csv
or
.Cm tsv
file is read back with
.Fl I
and the predicate's
.Fl T ,
which says what the format does not.
.Pp
Exports are written after the queries and before an interactive session.
Each file is created or truncated only once the relation is known, when
its first row is written or, for an empty relation, when its query
succeeds, so a run that fails before then, in evaluation at a limit
included, leaves the file as it was.
A write that fails leaves the rows written before it and exits 1.
.Sh RESOURCE LIMITS
Each
.Fl l
limit is a decimal
.Ar value
from 0, which asks for the default, to the largest both a size and a
.Vt long long
can hold, 9223372036854775807 on a 64-bit system.
.Bl -tag -width memory
.It Cm tuples
the most tuples all relations together may hold, the program's facts
included; by default there is no limit.
.It Cm memory
the most bytes relation and index storage, the program's facts included,
the table of the integers the program states and evaluation computes, the
symbols and strings the program states and evaluation computes, the
buffers an evaluation computes strings in, the plans of an evaluation and
the rewrite of a query may occupy together; by default there is no limit.
A long rule's plans can need more memory than the data does.
The limit is not a bound on all the memory
.Nm
uses: the text being loaded and the field of a table being imported, which
.Cm input
bounds, and the program's rules and queries are sized by what was written,
and growing an array holds the old one until the new one is filled.
Without a memory limit, serial evaluation can use a bounded duplicate cache
to accelerate dense two-column relations.
A finite memory limit disables that cache to preserve the ordinary refusal
boundary, so those evaluations can take longer even with a generous limit.
.It Cm input
the most bytes one input may hold: a program file, or the program read
from the standard input, and one field of a table imported with
.Fl I ,
once its quoting or escapes are decoded; by default there is no limit.
A file is read no further than one byte past the limit, and a quoted field
left open holds no more than the limit before it is refused, located at
its opening quote.
.It Cm magic
the most work the Magic rewrite of one query may do: the body literals it
reads while it searches for what to generate and those of every rule it
generates, a literal with an expression counting one more per operator,
call and operand, and the names of the predicates it generates, a literal
per 64 bytes.
By default it is in proportion to the size of the program and the query,
large enough for every rewrite that specializes the program rather than
multiplies it.
.El
.Pp
A query or evaluation that exceeds
.Cm tuples
or
.Cm memory
fails, and writes no rows.
A file or a table that would exceed either is refused when it is loaded,
with nothing of it added, and is parsed no further than the first fact
shown not to fit.
When its facts are what exceed the limit, the diagnostic names the first
predicate, in the order the file names them, that the facts read would add
to:
.Dl relic: error: limit exceeded: tuples=4 while loading e/1
A file or a table that exceeds
.Cm input
is refused the same way, with nothing of it added:
.Dl relic: error: limit exceeded: input=1024 while loading graph.dl
.Cm magic
differs from the other limits: a rewrite that outgrows it, by default or
as set, is abandoned with a warning and the query answered from the whole
program, which never answers differently but may meet an arithmetic or
string error the rewrite would not have.
No value removes the limit; a value only replaces the default.
.Sh INTERACTIVE MODE
With
.Fl i ,
each line of the standard input is one of:
.Bl -bullet
.It
nothing but blanks, or a comment beginning with
.Ql % ,
which is ignored;
.It
a command beginning with a period;
.It
one query, which must fit on the line.
.El
.Pp
A query is answered as
.Fl q
answers one, into the same output and format.
Only queries are accepted; facts and rules are not.
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width ".plan query"
.It Ic .help
List the commands.
.It Ic .plan Ar query
Run
.Ar query
and write its plan instead of its rows, to the standard output even under
.Fl e .
.It Ic .quit
End the session.
.It Ic .stats
Write the evaluation counters.
.El
.Pp
A command takes nothing after its name but
.Ic .plan ,
and a line beginning with a period that names no command is refused.
A command's reply is written to the standard output, never to the
.Fl o
file.
Each query of the session, and each
.Ic .plan ,
is numbered after the queries that ran before the session, and a query
that is rejected still takes its number.
A query or command that fails is reported, and the session goes on.
The session ends at the end of input, at
.Ic .quit ,
or when a read or a write fails.
.Pp
When the standard input and the standard error are both a terminal, the
session prompts with
.Ql relic>
on the standard error and, where
.Nm
was built with libedit, allows each line to be edited and recalled.
Otherwise there is no prompt, so that a session read from a file or a pipe
writes its answers and nothing else.
Everything answered is written out before the session waits for the next
line.
.Pp
In a session that prompts, an interrupt from the terminal, usually
control-C, while a line is answered stops its query, which fails with the
diagnostic
.Qq interrupted ,
and the session goes on to the next line.
Rows written before the interrupt stay written; the
.Cm json
format's closing bracket is not.
An interrupt does not end a write that blocks, such as one to a pipe whose
reader has stopped reading.
While the session waits for a line, and at any time in a run that does not
prompt, an interrupt ends
.Nm
as it ends any program.
.Sh OUTPUT
Every format writes one row per answer.
A row's columns are the query's variables, in the order they first occur,
but for
.Ql _
and the variables of an aggregate's braces.
A query with no variables writes one empty row when it holds and nothing
when it does not.
In
.Cm text ,
.Cm csv
and
.Cm tsv ,
the rows of each query but the first are preceded by an empty line, except
in an interactive session whose prompt and results share a terminal, where
the prompt marks where each query's rows begin.
The empty row and that empty line look alike, so successive queries with no
variables show how many of them held but not which;
.Cm json
tells them apart.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm text
Values separated by one space, each written as a program would write it:
integers in decimal, symbols as they are, and strings in double quotes with
the escapes of the language.
A string's control characters, C1 controls included, and any byte that is
not valid UTF-8 are escaped, so results are always safe to print.
.It Cm csv
RFC 4180 fields separated by commas, each record ending in a newline.
A field is quoted when it holds a comma, a double quote, a carriage return
or a newline, or is empty.
Symbols and strings are written as their bytes, unescaped, except at a
terminal, described below.
.It Cm tsv
Fields separated by one tab.
A backslash, tab, newline and carriage return inside a value are written as
.Ql \e\e ,
.Ql \et ,
.Ql \en
and
.Ql \er ,
and every other byte as it is, except at a terminal.
.It Cm json
One document per query: an array of rows, each an array of values, one row
per line.
An integer is a number and a symbol a string holding its bytes; a string
value is a JSON string holding the
.Cm text
format's quoted literal, so a JSON string beginning with a double quote is
a string value and any other is a symbol.
.El
.Pp
Written to a file or a pipe,
.Cm csv
and
.Cm tsv
hold each value's bytes, so a table written with them reads back with
.Fl I
as the same values.
Written to a terminal, standard output or a file named by
.Fl o
or
.Fl O
that is one, they write every byte of a value that is not part of a
printable character as
.Ql \exHH ,
by the rule of the
.Cm text
format, so that results are safe to print there too:
.Cm tsv
keeps its own escapes, and
.Cm csv
leaves a backslash as it is.
.Pp
Diagnostics, plans, counts and counters are written to the standard error,
so the standard output holds only results and, in an interactive session,
the replies to its commands.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width LC_CTYPE
.It Ev LC_ALL , LC_CTYPE , LANG
The character encoding of the lines an interactive session edits, chosen
as
.Xr setlocale 3
chooses it, the first of these that is set and not empty deciding; ASCII
when none is.
Bytes typed that are not text in the encoding are dropped, or the line
holding them is refused, as libedit chooses; a refused line takes with it
whatever had been typed or pasted after it and not yet read.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ~/.editrc
.It Pa ~/.editrc
libedit's configuration, read when an interactive session edits its lines;
see
.Xr editrc 5 .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
It exits 1 when the program or a query was rejected, when evaluation failed,
when anything in an interactive session failed, when input or output
failed, or when the library could not start for want of memory or of a
random device, and 2 when the command line is malformed or asks to read a
program or a table from standard input when it is a terminal.
Warnings do not change the status.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check a program without evaluating it:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic -c project.dl
.Pp
Answer the queries written in two files that form one program:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic rules.dl facts.dl
.Pp
Answer a query given on the command line, as CSV:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic -F csv -q 'depends(X, Y)?' project.dl
.Pp
Show how a query is answered, without its rows:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic -n -e -q 'path(a, X)?' graph.dl
.Pp
Bound the work a query may do:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic -l tuples=5000000 -l memory=2147483648 -q 'path(X, Y)?' graph.dl
.Pp
Answer a program's queries over a table of edges, and over a CSV file with a
header whose second column is not needed:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic -T edge:symbol,symbol -I edge.tsv reach.dl
.Dl $ relic -T person:symbol,_,integer -I csv,header:person=people.csv rules.dl
.Pp
Write a derived relation to a table, and as facts a later run loads back:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic -a -O path.tsv -O path.dl graph.dl
.Dl $ relic -O path.dl graph.dl && relic -q 'path(a, X)?' path.dl
.Pp
Query a table read from the standard input, with no program file:
.Pp
.Dl $ relic -T edge:symbol,symbol -I tsv:edge=- -q 'edge(a, X)?' < edge.tsv
.Pp
Explore a program interactively:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ relic -i graph.dl
relic> path(a, X)?
b
c
relic> .quit
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
A diagnostic about the program or a table names the file, line and column it
concerns, as
.Ql graph.dl:4:22: error: message ,
and is an
.Ql error ,
a
.Ql warning ,
or a
.Ql note
adding to the one before it.
The standard input is named
.Ql <stdin> .
A diagnostic about anything else begins with the program's name, as
.Ql relic: error: message .
Any byte that is not printable ASCII in a diagnostic is escaped.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr relic 3 ,
.Xr editrc 5
.Sh CAVEATS
.Cm csv
and
.Cm tsv
write a string's bytes as they are, control characters included.
They are meant for programs to read; the results of an untrusted program in
either format, printed to a terminal, can carry escape sequences.
